Let koko-aio read rotation from the Rotation uniform

koko-aio decides whether the picture is rotated in one of two ways. With
_HAS_ROTATION_UNIFORM defined it reads the Rotation uniform, which RetroArch
1.16 and later provide, and so does this layer. Without it, it falls back to a
guess: a pass whose OutputSize has a different aspect from FinalViewportSize is
taken to be rotated. The fallback says of itself that it "only works if the
calling pass scales to viewport".

The libretro copy ships with the define commented out, for the sake of older
RetroArch versions, so every pass takes the guess. The ambient light pass,
ambi_temporal_pass, is a fixed 96x64. Unless the viewport is also exactly 3:2,
it concludes the game is rotated and maps the region "under the tube" -- the
part it leaves black -- as though the picture were turned: a narrow band across
the whole width. On a 16:9 or 21:9 screen that band pokes out of both sides of
the bezel as two hard black rectangles in the middle of the ambient glow.

The Rotation uniform is there, so use it, as the comment beside the define
asks of any RetroArch newer than 1.16.

--- a/bezel/koko-aio/config/config-static.inc
+++ b/bezel/koko-aio/config/config-static.inc
@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@
 
 
 	// In case you're running an old Retrtoarch version (pre 1.16), please comment this (prepend: // )
-//#define _HAS_ROTATION_UNIFORM
+#define _HAS_ROTATION_UNIFORM
 
 #define D3D_WORKAROUND
 
